 [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "23 – 27 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/788"} "Olympiako Kopelatodramio Skhoinia, Schinias National Park"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/256346"} "32 from 16 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10789"} "Henrik Nilsson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10796"} "Markus Oscarsson"] ", the 2000 Olympic runners-up, had won the K-2 1000 at the last two editions of the World Championships, as well as the 2002 European Championships, and were the favorites in the event at the 2004 Games. Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/90226"} "Eirik Verås Larsen"] "  and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90225"} "Nils Olav Fjeldheim"] " were also a strong duo, having won the 2001 and 2004 European and 2001 World Championships and taken silver and bronze at the 2002 Worlds and Europeans respectively. Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10159"} "Beniamino Bonomi"] " were the defending Olympic champions, but had not medalled at a major international event since. Sweden and Norway won their opening heats, with the latter posting a slightly faster time, and qualified directly for the final, while Italy was sixth in Sweden’s heat and qualified for the final only by placing second in the semi-finals, behind Belgium’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/105222"} "Wouter D’Haene"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90127"} "Bob Maesen"] ", the 2003 World runners-up. Sweden took command in the final and won gold with by over a second ahead of its nearest competitor. Italy, meanwhile, had to fend off Norway in order to take silver, which it did by a margin of only 0.044 seconds. Norway had slightly more breathing room, 0.428 seconds, over Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/105212"} "David Rhodes"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/8998"} "Danny Collins"] ", the latter of whom was a bronze and silver medalist in the K-2 500 at the 1996 and 2000 Olympics respectively. Larsen thus added bronze to the gold medal he had won in the K-1 1000 earlier in the day."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "217/218"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10796"} "Markus Oscarsson"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/10789"} "Henrik Nilsson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "3:09.536 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:18.420 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "141/142"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/10159"} "Beniamino Bonomi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "3:14.060 (6 h1)"] [:td {} "3:12.597 (2)"] [:td {} "3:19.484 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "155/156"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/90226"} "Eirik Verås Larsen"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/90225"} "Nils Olav Fjeldheim"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "3:09.247 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:19.528 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "9/10"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8998"} "Danny Collins"]